{"is_error":false,"duration_api_ms":246692,"num_turns":1,"stop_reason":"end_turn","session_id":"33ee9e36-88de-40ce-98e4-946913b7efea","total_cost_usd":1.181334,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":60626,"cache_read_input_tokens":20196,"output_tokens":20857,"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":60626,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0},"inference_geo":"not_available","iterations":[{"input_tokens":2,"output_tokens":20857,"cache_read_input_tokens":20196,"cache_creation_input_tokens":60626,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":60626},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard"},"modelUsage":{"claude-haiku-4-5-20251001":{"inputTokens":43431,"outputTokens":22,"cacheReadInputTokens":0,"cacheCreationInputTokens":0,"webSearchRequests":0,"costUSD":0.043540999999999996,"contextWindow":200000,"maxOutputTokens":32000,"canonicalModel":"claude-haiku-4-5","provider":"firstParty"},"claude-opus-5":{"inputTokens":2,"outputTokens":20857,"cacheReadInputTokens":20196,"cacheCreationInputTokens":60626,"webSearchRequests":0,"costUSD":1.137793,"contextWindow":1000000,"maxOutputTokens":64000,"canonicalModel":"claude-opus-5","provider":"firstParty"}},"permission_denials":[],"terminal_reason":"completed","fast_mode_state":"off","fast_mode_disabled_reason":"sdk_opt_in_required","subtype":"success","api_error_status":null,"result":"{\n  \"agent\": \"spec-auditor\",\n  \"verdict\": \"fail\",\n  \"summary\": \"Fourteen of sixteen checks pass and the catalog is internally clean, but the two rules that decide how a run ends both fail in flight: the cheapest descent is a single plunge in all nine measured descents despite a published cost curve whose minimum is 3, and no descent lands soft — touchdown is 21–23 m/s because the parachute never opens, which makes the 4.59 m/s full-hold figure and the whole of GDD 2.3.2 inert as flown.\",\n  \"checks\": [\n    {\n      \"rule_id\": \"band_names_consistent\",\n      \"gdd_ref\": \"2.3.7\",\n      \"statement\": \"Every band name used in the catalog or the params appears in the baseline's bands, and no band is referenced that the baseline does not define.\",\n      \"result\": \"pass\",\n      \"evidence\": \"baseline.bands = {suborbital, low, high}. Catalog uses exactly {suborbital, low, high} across all 27 debris entries and all 3 band_summary entries. Params reference bands in five places — ablation.fixed_toll_per_pass_pct_by_band, ablation.heat_index, ablation.cost_curve, ablation.optimal_pass_count, economy.band_value_multiplier — and each carries exactly those three keys, no more and no fewer. No orphan name in either direction.\",\n      \"fix_hint\": \"\",\n      \"owner\": \"debris-designer\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"rule_id\": \"band_summary_matches_debris\",\n      \"gdd_ref\": \"2.3.7\",\n      \"statement\": \"Every band_summary entry agrees with the debris array it summarises: piece_count, mean_mass_kg and fragile_fraction all recomputed.\",\n      \"result\": \"pass\",\n      \"evidence\": \"Suborbital: 9 pieces, masses 221+78+182+42.25+58.5+26+52+35.75+600 = 1295.5, /9 = 143.944 vs stated 143.94; spawn weights sum 70, fragile weights 4+3 = 7, 7/70 = 0.100 vs stated 0.1. Low: 9 pieces, 403+312+143+117+169+71.5+357.5+123.5+750 = 2446.5, /9 = 271.833 vs 271.83; weights sum 60, fragile 5+3+2 = 10, 10/60 = 0.1667 vs 0.167. High: 9 pieces, 624+320+286+507+136.5+290+377+442+950 = 3932.5, /9 = 436.944 vs 436.94; weights sum 64, fragile 6+5+4+1 = 16, 16/64 = 0.250 vs 0.25. All three summaries reproduce from the array; fragile_fraction is spawn-weighted, which is the share the player actually meets.\",\n      \"fix_hint\": \"\",\n      \"owner\": \"debris-designer\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"rule_id\": \"mean_mass_rises_by_band\",\n      \"gdd_ref\": \"2.3.7\",\n      \"statement\": \"The catalog's mean piece mass rises band over band — valuable junk physically fights you.\",\n      \"result\": \"pass\",\n      \"evidence\": \"143.94 -> 271.83 -> 436.94 kg, a 1.89x step then a 1.61x step, 3.04x end to end. Excluding the one oversized outlier per band, the hand-tetherable population runs 86.94 / 212.06 / 372.81 kg (4.29x). Measured in the unit the player meets — spawn-weighted kg per crushed slot, fragile counted uncrushed — it is 5700.5/84 = 67.9, 14020.5/111 = 126.3, and 196.6 kg/slot. Monotone by every measure.\",\n      \"fix_hint\": \"\",\n      \"owner\": \"debris-designer\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"rule_id\": \"fragile_share_rises_by_band\",\n      \"gdd_ref\": \"2.3.7\",\n      \"statement\": \"Fragile spawn share rises across the bands, roughly 1 in 10 suborbital to 1 in 4 high.\",\n      \"result\": \"pass\",\n      \"evidence\": \"Spawn-weighted: 7/70 = 0.100 (exactly 1 in 10), 10/60 = 0.167 (1 in 6), 16/64 = 0.250 (exactly 1 in 4). Monotone and it lands on both endpoints the GDD names. Fragile types also carry below-average weights inside their own band (e.g. low band fragile weights 5/3/2 against a 6.67 band mean), so variety rises faster than encounter rate.\",\n      \"fix_hint\": \"\",\n      \"owner\": \"debris-designer\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"rule_id\": \"full_hold_doubles_mass\",\n      \"gdd_ref\": \"2.3.5\",\n      \"statement\": \"A full hold roughly doubles the rocket's mass at starting storage size.\",\n      \"result\": \"pass\",\n      \"evidence\": \"Spawn-weighted across the shipping slice's hand-tetherable population (fragile pieces charged their uncrushed slot cost, since they never crush): suborbital 5700.5 kg over 84 slots and low 14020.5 kg over 111 slots gives 19721/195 = 101.13 kg per slot. base_slots 6 x 101.13 = 606.8 kg against dry_mass_kg 500 -> (500+606.8)/500 = 2.21x. Inside 25% of doubling (1.75-2.25) but at the top of it, with 0.04 to spare. The 500 kg dry mass is not a convenience: it is the value baseline.json assumed, and it is the same value the staged ballistic coefficient 500/(1.45*3.5) = 98.5 kg/m2 requires, so two independent constraints pin it.\",\n      \"fix_hint\": \"\",\n      \"owner\": \"economy-balancer\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"rule_id\": \"ablation_optimum_two_to_four_passes\",\n      \"gdd_ref\": \"2.3.1\",\n      \"statement\": \"The two ablation knobs are tuned so the cheapest descent is 2-4 committed passes from every band — never a single plunge and never feathering.\",\n      \"result\": \"fail\",\n      \"evidence\": \"The published curve is internally sound. Recomputing cost(n) = n*toll + 0.00074*n*(heat_index/n)^2.2: suborbital n=1 = 2.0 + 0.00074*25118.9 = 20.59 (array 20.59), n=3 = 6.0 + 0.00222*2240.4 = 10.97 (array 10.97); low n=2 = 4.6 + 0.00148*6661.5 = 14.46 (array 14.46), n=3 = 6.9 + 0.00222*2730.1 = 12.96 (array 12.96); high n=1 = 2.9 + 0.00074*42645 = 34.46 (array 34.46), n=3 = 8.7 + 0.00222*3803.6 = 17.14 (array 17.14). All six recomputations match to the stated digit, all three minima sit at n=3, and each matches optimal_pass_count. The rule still fails, because the flight does not run the array: measured cheapest_pass_count is 1 in 9 of 9 descents and plate cost is monotone increasing in n everywhere — suborbital empty 20.6 / 25.9 / 28.3 / 56.5% at 1 / 2 / 3 / 16 passes, low full hold 50.4 -> 70.7% across 1-6, high full hold 49.8 -> 91.7% across 1-9. The mechanism is measured, not inferred: the one-pass plunge peaks 100 while the shallowest two-pass descent peaks 106, so within a 90 m atmosphere carrying a 5 s heat time constant a shallow pass is hotter than a steep one. With cost = toll + C*peak^E, every alternative to n=1 adds a positive toll to a strictly hotter final pass, so no C > 0, E >= 1 or toll > 0 can invert the ordering. The curve claims 2-4 and the game delivers 1.\",\n      \"fix_hint\": \"No value in the ablation block can fix this — the lever is the descent profile (passes that lower apoapsis and therefore entry speed between them, so the final pass is cooler than the plunge), failing which GDD 4.5 risk 1's own named fallback of a flat per-pass cost with a hard pass cap, which also means relaxing the schema field that requires optimal_pass_count in 2-4.\",\n      \"owner\": \"economy-balancer\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"rule_id\": \"parachute_full_hold_soft_landing\",\n      \"gdd_ref\": \"2.3.2\",\n      \"statement\": \"Parachute descent speed at full hold is under the 5 m/s soft-landing threshold and above about 3.5 m/s, so the Parachute upgrade is a real purchase.\",\n      \"result\": \"fail\",\n      \"evidence\": \"The stated terminal velocity checks out and is now independently computable, which is an improvement: full hold 500+606.8 = 1106.8 kg gives sqrt(2*1106.8*30/(3.0*1.5*700)) = sqrt(21.082) = 4.59 m/s, under 5 with 0.41 to spare and above 3.5; empty gives sqrt(30000/3150) = 3.09 m/s, and 4.59/3.09 = 1.488 = sqrt(1106.8/500), so the square-root-of-mass rule holds on its own. Parachute tier 1 (Cd 1.5 -> 2.1) takes full hold to 3.88 m/s, a real purchase. But the flight never reaches terminal velocity: measured touchdown is 21.03-23.34 m/s vertical in all 9 descents, soft_landing is false in all 9, and the parachute deployed in none of them, because the ship still carries most of its orbital horizontal velocity at contact so total speed never falls under the 58 m/s plasma gate that arms the chute. As flown, 4.59 m/s is a number no landing produces, and every rule downstream of it — the 5 m/s grade line, no_gear_multiplier 2, fragile_multiplier 2, and the entire Cd 1.5/2.1/2.9 upgrade line — never fires. The win condition (set Armstrong's satellite down gently) rests on this.\",\n      \"fix_hint\": \"Not fixable in landing.parachute_area_m2 — the chute deploy gate must be armed off atmosphere-relative or vertical speed rather than total speed, or the descent profile must shed horizontal velocity before contact; until then landing.descent_speed_full_hold_ms is an assertion the flight contradicts and should not be published as a measurement.\",\n      \"owner\": \"economy-balancer\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"rule_id\": \"tow_fee_clamps_and_is_linear\",\n      \"gdd_ref\": \"2.3.3\",\n      \"statement\": \"The tow fee is zero inside the free radius, linear beyond it, clamps at exactly 50%, and can never go negative or exceed the cap.\",\n      \"result\": \"pass\",\n      \"evidence\": \"free_radius_m 100, max_fee_fraction 0.5, curve linear. Half the circumference is pi*800 = 2513.3 m, so fee(d) = 0 for d <= 100 and 0.5*(d-100)/2413.3 beyond, reaching exactly 0.500 at d = 2513.3 m — the far side of the planet, as 2.3.3 requires. The form is monotone non-decreasing from a floor of 0, so it cannot go negative, and it is clamped at the cap, so payout is 100% at the pad and never below 50% anywhere. The free zone at 100 m is 2.0% of the 5026.5 m circumference, a plausible one-screen pad.\",\n      \"fix_hint\": \"\",\n      \"owner\": \"economy-balancer\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"rule_id\": \"lazy_run_breaks_even\",\n      \"gdd_ref\": \"2.3.6\",\n      \"statement\": \"Launch cost is at or below the value of three cheap suborbital pieces, so a lazy run still breaks even.\",\n      \"result\": \"pass\",\n      \"evidence\": \"size_class_base_value.small 60 x band_value_multiplier.suborbital 1.0 = 60 per cheap piece; three of them = 180 against launch_cost 140, clearing the stage-and-tank bill by 40 (1.29x). The modal suborbital haul — 6 crushed smalls — returns 360, or 2.57x the launch cost. Re-plating at 2 per percent bills 41 on the cheapest flown suborbital descent (20.6% plate), so a clean run still nets 179 on the modal haul and sloppy flying is visibly more expensive without a lazy run ever losing money.\",\n      \"fix_hint\": \"\",\n      \"owner\": \"economy-balancer\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"rule_id\": \"steady_tow_never_shears\",\n      \"gdd_ref\": \"2.3.4\",\n      \"statement\": \"Steady-state towing never shears a magnet: per-cable tension at the heaviest legal load stays below magnet_hold_force_n with margin.\",\n      \"result\": \"pass\",\n      \"evidence\": \"Heaviest legal two-tether load in the shipping slice (base_tether_slots 2, hand-tetherable only): satellite_bus_frame 403 kg + thruster_quad_cluster 357.5 kg = 760.5 kg. Astronaut acceleration = jetpack_thrust_n 180 / (suit_mass_kg 45 + 760.5) = 0.2235 m/s2. Worst cable tension = 403 x 0.2235 = 90.1 N against magnet_hold_force_n 450 — a 5.0x margin. The rule also holds structurally: adding mass lowers the shared acceleration, so per-cable tension falls as the string grows. A single 403 kg tether is the true worst case at 403 x 180/448 = 161.9 N, still 2.8x under the hold force. Shear is therefore reachable only through latch jerk and swing jerk, which are player inputs, as 2.3.4 requires.\",\n      \"fix_hint\": \"\",\n      \"owner\": \"economy-balancer\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"rule_id\": \"staging_is_one_way\",\n      \"gdd_ref\": \"2.3.1\",\n      \"statement\": \"No parameter anywhere reintroduces thrust after staging.\",\n      \"result\": \"pass\",\n      \"evidence\": \"The only thrust terms in the params are flight.thrust_n 38400 and flight.rcs_thrust_n 1700, both attached to the stage that staging jettisons, and eva.jetpack_thrust_n 180. No post-stage thrust field, no residual-fuel field, no upgrade whose effect is a thrust value applied after staging (the two thruster tiers move flight.thrust_n only). The jetpack is the GDD-sanctioned get-out-and-push and cannot substitute for an engine: 180 N on a 1106.8 kg full-hold ship is 0.163 m/s2, versus 38400/1106.8 = 34.7 m/s2 from the main thruster — 0.5% of staged thrust, a rescue nudge and not a landing.\",\n      \"fix_hint\": \"\",\n      \"owner\": \"economy-balancer\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"rule_id\": \"fragile_never_crushable_and_premium\",\n      \"gdd_ref\": \"2.3.4\",\n      \"statement\": \"Fragile is never crushable at any compactor tier present in the params, and the fragile premium makes one intact fragile piece worth several solid pieces of the same class and band.\",\n      \"result\": \"pass\",\n      \"evidence\": \"cargo.compactor_tier is 1, which crushes small only; the params contain no tier field or override that admits a fragile piece to the compactor at any tier, and the catalog's fragile flag is carried per piece independently of size_class. Premium: fragile_value_premium 3, so a low-band fragile small (star_tracker_head) is 60 x 2.6 x 3 = 468 against a solid low-band small at 156 — exactly 3 solid pieces of the same class and band, and a low-band fragile medium (cracked_solar_array) is 130 x 2.6 x 3 = 1014 against 338. Three-for-one clears 'several' at the piece level.\",\n      \"fix_hint\": \"\",\n      \"owner\": \"economy-balancer\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"rule_id\": \"oversized_rejects_hand_magnet\",\n      \"gdd_ref\": \"2.3.4\",\n      \"statement\": \"Oversized junk rejects the hand magnet — hand_tetherable is false for that class.\",\n      \"result\": \"pass\",\n      \"evidence\": \"size_classes.oversized.hand_tetherable = false; small and medium are both true. All three oversized pieces carry that class and nothing else — derelict_upper_stage 600 kg (suborbital), collapsed_boom_mast 750 kg (low), armstrongs_satellite 950 kg (high) — each at spawn_weight 1, and slot costs 6 crushed / 12 uncrushed. No piece outside the oversized class is marked non-tetherable and no oversized piece is marked tetherable, so the class boundary and the tool boundary are the same line.\",\n      \"fix_hint\": \"\",\n      \"owner\": \"debris-designer\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"rule_id\": \"twelve_upgrade_purchases\",\n      \"gdd_ref\": \"4.1\",\n      \"statement\": \"Twelve upgrade purchases: six parts, two tiers each, tier 2 costing more than tier 1 for the same part, each tier improving the stat it names.\",\n      \"result\": \"pass\",\n      \"evidence\": \"12 entries over exactly the six slice parts — fuel_tank, thruster, storage, heat_shield, parachute, hand_magnet — two tiers each. Costs rise within every part: 400->1300, 450->1450, 500->1600, 550->1700, 420->1350, 380->1250. Stats improve in the right direction from the base params: fuel_capacity_kg 300->415->560, thrust_n 38400->49400->63100, base_slots 6->9->12, plate_capacity_pct 100->150->210, parachute_drag_coefficient 1.5->2.1->2.9 (higher Cd = slower descent: 4.59 -> 3.88 -> 3.30 m/s at full hold), and hand_magnet magnet_range_screens 0.125->0.1875 at tier 1 then magnet_hold_force_n 450->750 at tier 2. Total spend 11,300.\",\n      \"fix_hint\": \"\",\n      \"owner\": \"economy-balancer\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"rule_id\": \"shipping_slice_bands_populated\",\n      \"gdd_ref\": \"4.1\",\n      \"statement\": \"Both slice bands carry at least one fragile piece and at least two size classes, so the player meets crushing and meets fragility inside suborbital and low.\",\n      \"result\": \"pass\",\n      \"evidence\": \"Suborbital: 9 pieces spanning small (6), medium (1) and oversized (1... 8 tetherable of 9), with two fragile — cracked_antenna_dish 58.5 kg and camera_optics_pod 35.75 kg, 7 of 70 spawn weight. Low: 9 pieces spanning small (3), medium (5) and oversized (1), with three fragile — cracked_solar_array 143, star_tracker_head 71.5, dish_reflector_petal 123.5, 10 of 60 spawn weight. Both bands contain crushable smalls, so compactor_tier 1 does visible work in both, and both contain a fragile piece that tier 1 must refuse, so the exception is teachable below 400 m. The three crane-only pieces are present and unreachable, which is the intended visible-but-deferred state.\",\n      \"fix_hint\": \"\",\n      \"owner\": \"debris-designer\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"rule_id\": \"no_target_moved_to_satisfy_a_rule\",\n      \"gdd_ref\": \"2.2\",\n      \"statement\": \"No rule passes only because a quantity the design document fixes in prose was moved.\",\n      \"result\": \"pass\",\n      \"evidence\": \"Every prose-fixed number is intact: cargo.base_slots 6 (GDD 2.2 'Cargo reads 4 of 6' and the appendix's open question), eva.base_tether_slots 2 (2.3.4), eva.magnet_range_screens 0.125 (~1/8 screen, 2.3.4), reentry.heat_dissipation_s 5 and cargo_damage_interval_s 3 and heat_capacity 100 (2.3.1), landing.soft_landing_ms 5 with no_gear_multiplier 2 and fragile_multiplier 2 (2.3.2), tow_fee.max_fee_fraction 0.5 (2.3.3), cargo.compactor_tier 1 (4.1). dry_mass_kg moved 80 -> 500, but toward the value baseline.json states rather than away from any GDD prose, and it is independently pinned by the staged ballistic coefficient 500/(1.45*3.5) = 98.5 kg/m2, so it is not a widened target. ablation.heat_index is no longer copied from the flight — anchored to baseline entry speeds 151.1/155.7/163.7 m/s cubed it gives 100/109.4/127.2, and the proof it is independent is that it openly disagrees with the measured single-pass peaks of 100/103/105. Two published values remain assertions the flight contradicts rather than measurements (ablation.cost_curve/optimal_pass_count and landing.descent_speed_full_hold_ms); both are failed above on their own rules rather than double-counted here.\",\n      \"fix_hint\": \"\",\n      \"owner\": \"economy-balancer\"\n    }\n  ],\n  \"observations\": [\n    \"The heat bar is calibrated so that the easiest possible descent — empty ship, suborbital, one pass — peaks at exactly 100, which is also reentry.heat_capacity. That makes the cargo-damage line equal to the best case: measured peak heat exceeds 100 in 6 of 9 descents (128 and 150 suborbital half/full, 133 and 154 low, 133 and 152 high, reaching 158 on deep multi-pass entries), so every loaded reentry in the shipping slice is over the line the design says destroys a cargo slot every 3 seconds. The sweep prices shield plate only and never charges that damage, which is why all nine still report landed = true.\",\n    \"Neither heat_shield tier touches heat_capacity — both move plate_capacity_pct only (100 -> 150 -> 210). GDD 2.3.1 says heat shield upgrades buy margin; as parameterised they buy passes, not peak. A player who keeps overheating cannot spend their way to a cooler reentry, only to more of them.\",\n    \"suit_mass_kg 45 against the catalog's post-rescale masses makes the astronaut a parked car rather than the tugboat 2.3.4 asks for. One low-band medium on the tether drops acceleration from 180/45 = 4.00 m/s2 to 180/448 = 0.402 m/s2, 10% of unloaded, and the heaviest legal pair drops it to 0.2235 m/s2, 5.6%. The greed decision collapses at the first piece, because the second one barely changes an already-immovable load. The Debris Designer declared this consequence and the Balancer accepted it as theirs to fix; it is still unfixed.\",\n    \"The band difficulty curve is carried entirely by cargo mass, not altitude. Empty single-pass peak heat is 100 / 103 / 105 across the three bands (a 5% spread) and cheapest plate cost 20.6 / 22.3 / 23.6% (15%), against ablation.heat_index's claimed 27% — because entry speeds are trapped between circular and escape speed at 151-164 m/s. Holding band fixed, empty -> full hold moves cost 20.6 -> 47.1%, 22.3 -> 50.4%, 23.6 -> 49.8%. GDD 2.3.7's 'each altitude band up is disproportionately hotter to return from' is not true as flown; risk 2 is cleared by mass and by the band-gated tolls instead.\",\n    \"Fuel is not a decision in the slice. All three bands were reached with 55.6 / 51.7 / 46.7% of the tank left (167 / 155 / 140 kg of 300), roughly 5 s of remaining burn, so the fuel_tank line at 400 and 1300 buys capacity the flight profile does not consume — and 'run out of fuel in space' is one of the two loss conditions in section 1.\",\n    \"The fragile premium is 3x per piece but only about 1.5x per slot, because a fragile piece pays uncrushed cost forever: a low-band fragile small is 468 across 2 slots (234/slot) against a solid small at 156 across 1. With a 6-slot hold and double damage on heat and landing, an optimising player may reasonably decide to ignore the glint — which 2.3.7 names as the failure mode that wastes the mechanic.\",\n    \"hand_magnet's two tiers move different stats — tier 1 buys range, tier 2 buys hold force. A player who wants a stronger magnet has to buy 380 of range they may not want first, and the 1250 tier-2 hold-force purchase is aimed at a shear mode that steady towing never reaches (5.0x margin at the heaviest legal load), so its value depends entirely on how often jerk shear actually fires in the hands.\",\n    \"Cross-checking the two blocking failures against each other: with touchdown at 21-23 m/s in every descent, landing.damage_per_ms_over 6 would charge roughly 100 points of damage on a rule intended to price a metre-per-second of overspeed. Whatever fixes the chute gate should be flown before that coefficient is trusted, since it has never been exercised in its intended range.\"\n  ]\n}","ttft_ms":144188,"ttft_stream_ms":1877,"time_to_request_ms":213,"type":"result","duration_ms":244922,"uuid":"f8f14538-b784-43c3-91d2-50adf12993d7"}
